{
  "Security Type": "Input Validation",
  "Description": "The new `_get_environment_env_vars` method reads `etc/conda/env_vars` from the environment prefix and parses it line by line with `env_assignment.split(\"=\")`. The file content is treated as trusted and is not validated. The parsing is also naive. `parts[1]` keeps only the text before a second '=', so a value that contains '=' (a URL query string, a base64 value, a PATH-like value) is silently truncated. Keys are not checked at all, so a name can be empty, contain whitespace or shell metacharacters, or be a security-sensitive variable such as `PATH`, `LD_PRELOAD`, `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` or `PYTHONPATH`. The returned dictionary is meant to be exported into the user's shell on activation. Nothing restricts which variables may be set or checks that the names and values are well formed. The file is also opened without any exception handling.",
  "Impact": "Anyone who can write to or modify the env_vars file in a shared, packaged or downloaded environment can inject arbitrary environment variables into the activating user's shell. Setting `LD_PRELOAD`, `PATH` or similar variables can lead to code execution, hijacked binaries or altered behavior. If values or keys are later written into generated activation scripts without quoting, shell metacharacters could give command injection. Silent truncation of values containing '=' produces incorrect configuration, which can cause subtle misbehavior or security-relevant misconfiguration, such as broken credentials or truncated URLs. A malformed file can also cause errors or crashes during activation, for example through encoding or permission failures.",
